<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaki Sherocho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaki Sherocho was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. He resisted the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> until 1897, when Kaffa was conquered and he was captured and taken north. The kingdom was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, and he died in captivity years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaki-sherocho.jpg" alt="Gaki Sherocho" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Buffet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaki_Sherocho_in_ceremonial_dress.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Gaki Sherocho reigned as tato, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, from 6 April 1890. His people usually called him Chinito, the diminutive of Taten Chini, King Chini. According to informants questioned by Amnon Orent, he had more fields cleared from the forests than any of his predecessors and organised the districts of Kaffa to kill the wildlife that harmed crops and livestock. The same tradition says that he ruled with an iron hand and travelled widely in the countryside to enforce his laws.</p><p>In January 1897 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> sent three armies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, under Ras Walda Giyorgis, who had already been named governor of the kingdom, Dejazmach Demissew Nassibu and Dejazmach Tessema Nadew. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-jifar-ii">Abba Jifar II</a> of Jimma joined them with his own troops. Walda Giyorgis attacked from Konta in the south-east, which was less strongly fortified than the boundary with Jimma along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojeb-river">Gojeb River</a>. Against an army of 31,000 men, 20,000 of them with rifles, Gaki Sherocho could field about 300 obsolete firearms.</p><p>According to the historian Harold Marcus, he called up all men between the ages of eight and 80 for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> struggle. He buried his crown on Mount Butto, trusting a legend that the kingdom would not fall while the crown remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>. Alexander Bulatovich, who visited Kaffa and met Ethiopian participants, recorded that the king forbade the growing of crops, hoping that hunger would drive the invaders back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his capital Anderaccha fell, Gaki Sherocho eluded capture in the hinterland for nine months. One account explained this by his people's love for him, while Bahru Zewde described him as despotic and saw that as the cause of his downfall. Captured on 10 September 1897, he was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> in chains forged from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> looted from his own treasury and made to show his submission before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>.</p><p>He was then held prisoner at the former capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankober">Ankober</a>, and the crown, whose hiding place informants had been forced to reveal, was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> for fear that it might inspire a rising. Two laments ascribed to him mourn his captivity. After Menelik's death he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, where he died, leaving nine children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaki Sherocho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1897 Menelik II's armies conquered Kaffa after a long resistance, and its last king, <b>Gaki Sherocho</b>, was taken captive.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kaffa">Kingdom of Kaffa</a></li></ul></section>
